                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/physicscontents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ViaPerturbativeAlgebraicDeformationQuantization"} Via perturbative algebraic deformation quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#via_nonperturbative_geometric_quantization"} Via non-perturbative geometric quantization
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesPerturbativeAlgebraic"} On motivic structures in perturbative quantum field theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesNonPerturbativeGeometric"} On motivic structures in non-perturbative local quantum field theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motive"} Motivic
            structures enters
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            in two dual guises, related, on the one hand, to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+deformation+quantization"} algebraic deformation quantization
            and, on the other hand, to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %h3#ViaPerturbativeAlgebraicDeformationQuantization Via perturbative algebraic deformation quantization
          %p
            In the first case one observes that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            amounts to choosing an inverse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formality"} formality
            map from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/En-algebras"} En-algebras
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pn-algebras"} Pn-algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "deformation+quantization#MotivicGaloisGroup"} Motivic Galois group action on the space of quantizations
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+infinity-group"} automorphism infinity-group
            of either side therefore naturally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-action"} acts
            on the space of formal deformation quantization choices obtained this way and one shows (conjectured by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            (
            %a{:href => "#Kontsevich99"}> Kontsevich 99
            ), recently proven by Dolgushev) that the connected component group of this is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+group"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
            = succeed "\u2018" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acting
            on the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalizations
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            According to Kontsevich, this explains the role of motivic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+functions"} correlation functions
            and hence in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            (see also at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amplituhedron"} amplituhedron
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+field+theory"} perturbative field theory
          %p
            More concretely,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            typically are expressions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiple+zeta+values"} multiple zeta values
            and handling them involves intricate combinatorics. By just re-expressing these in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+multiple+zeta+values"} motivic multiple zeta values
            (see the rerferences) much of the combinatorics becomes more tractable (in terms of some
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives"} motives
            do not play much of a role in these computations, but one makes use of the combinatorial simplification obtained by re-expressing multiple zeta values by their motivic version.
          %p
            For more see the motive-related references at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+multiple+zeta+values"} motivic multiple zeta values
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+L-function"} motivic L-function
          %h3#via_nonperturbative_geometric_quantization Via non-perturbative geometric quantization
          %p
            On the other hand, in full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            in its modern cohomological form as
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+by+push-forward"} geometric quantization by push-forward
            one finds a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"}> cohesive
            ” form of actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic cohomology
            exhibited by “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive"} cohesive
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motives"} pure motives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            is exhibited by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            itself exhibited by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+bivariant+cohomology"} twisted bivariant cocycle
            on the correspondence space, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic path integral quantization
            of this corresponds to the induced pull-push index transform. See the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesNonPerturbativeGeometric"} references below
          %h2#references References
          %p We list references
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ReferencesPerturbativeAlgebraic"} on motives in perturbative algebraic quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ReferencesNonPerturbativeGeometric"} on motives in non-perturbative geometric quantization
          %h3#ReferencesPerturbativeAlgebraic On motivic structures in perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            The action of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"}> cosmic Galois group
            ”) on the space of choices in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            was first observed/conjectured in
          %ul#Kontsevich99
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Operads and Motives in Deformation Quantization
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9904055"} arXiv:math/9904055
          %p A quick and rough survey of this and vaguely related motivic structures in physics is in the appendix of
          %ul
            %li
              A. Rej,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em Motives, an introductory survey for physicists
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.its.caltech.edu/~matilde/ObiMotivesSurveyFinal.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A detailed review of the motivic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            on the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            procedures is in section 7 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noncommutative+Geometry%2C+Quantum+Fields+and+Motives"} Noncommutative Geometry, Quantum Fields and Motives
          %p
            Discussion of motivic structure in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            is also the lecture
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spencer+Bloch"}> Spencer Bloch
              ,
              %em Dilogarithm motives appearing in physics
              (
              %a{:href => "http://blip.tv/pifagorov/spencer-bloch-dilogarithm-motives-arising-in-physics-3854827"}> video
              ); S. Bloch, H. Esnault, D. Kreimer,
              %em On motives associated to graph polynomials
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~bloch/graphpoly050928b.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %em The elliptic dilogarithm for the sunset graph
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://preprints.ihes.fr/2013/P/P-13-24.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also the material linked at
          %ul
            %li
              Conference
              = succeed "," do
                %em Amplitudes and Periods
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://pagesperso.ihes.fr/~vanhove/QFT2012"} web
          %p
            More recent developments on motivic structures in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            include for instance
          %ul
            %li
              John Golden, Alexander B. Goncharov, Marcus Spradlin, Cristian Vergu, Anastasia Volovich,
              %em Motivic Amplitudes and Cluster Coordinates
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1305.1617"}> arXiv:1305.1617
              )
          %h3#ReferencesNonPerturbativeGeometric On motivic structures in non-perturbative local quantum field theory
          %p
            The formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
            as a passage to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive"} cohesive
            generalized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motives"} pure motives
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7930"}> arXiv:1310.7930
              )
          %p based on
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joost+Nuiten"}> Joost Nuiten
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/master+thesis+Nuiten"} Cohomological quantization of local prequantum boundary field theory
          %p
            The following is a list of “precursors” of aspects of the idea
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            as laid out there
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            The formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            map in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            is attributed to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raoul+Bott"} Raoul Bott
          %p
            The proposal that the natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            for geometric quantization are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            is due to
          %ul#Hoermander71
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fourier Integral Operators I.
              %strong 127
              (1971)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symplectic manifolds and their lagrangian submanifolds
              %strong 6
              (1971)
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+quantization"} semiclassical quantization
            the precursor to this are works of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maslov"} Maslov
            in 1960s (which are quite related to Hoermander’s and Sato’s work).
          %p
            With the recognition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            in the 1980s,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theory"} index theory
            (hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward in generalized cohomology
            to the point) was understood to be about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+functions"} partition functions
            of systems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            in
          %ul#Getzler83
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Alvarez-Gaum%C3%A9"}> Luis Alvarez-Gaumé
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Supersymmetry and the Atiyah-Singer index theorem
                = succeed ":2" do
                  %strong 90
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1103940278"} euclid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ezra+Getzler"}> Ezra Getzler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Pseudodifferential operators on supermanifolds and the Atiyah-Singer index theorem
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.northwestern.edu/~getzler/Papers/1103940796.pdf"} pdf
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ezra+Getzler"}> Ezra Getzler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A short proof of the Atiyah-Singer index theorem
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://math.northwestern.edu/~getzler/Papers/local.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            In higher analogy to this but much more subtly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward
            to the point in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Ando"}> Matthew Ando
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Mike Hopkins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"} Charles Rezk
              = succeed "," do
                %em Multiplicative orientations of KO-theory and the spectrum of topological modular forms
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/~mando/papers/koandtmf.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The general perspective of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+as+a+pull-push+transform"} path integral as a pull-push transform
            was originally laid out, somewhat implicitly, in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum groups from path integrals
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://xxx.lanl.gov/abs/q-alg/9501025"} arXiv:q-alg/9501025
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher algebraic structures and quantization
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9212115"} arXiv:hep-th/9212115
          %p and then fully explicitly in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Twisted K-theory and the Verlinde ring
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ma.utexas.edu/users/dafr/Andrejewski%20Lectures.html"} pdf slides
          %p
            Discussion along these lines of a pull-push quantization over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            of a 2-dimensional
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#FreedHopkinsTeleman07
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hopkins"} Michael Hopkins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
              %em Consistent Orientation of Moduli Spaces
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0711.1909"}> arXiv:0711.1909
              ), chapter XIX, pages 395-419 in: Oscar Garcia-Prada, Jean Pierre Bourguignon, Simon Salamon (eds.)
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Many Facets of Geometry: A Tribute to Nigel Hitchin
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.oxfordscholarship.com/view/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199534920.001.0001/acprof-9780199534920"} doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199534920.001.0001
          %p
            More in detail a functorial quantization of suitable correspondences of smooth manifolds to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            by pull-push has been given in (
            %a{:href => "#ConnesSkandalis84"}> Connes-Skandalis 84
            ) and the generalization of that to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            (hence to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+K-theory"} groupoid K-theory
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoids"} action groupoids
          %ul#EmersonMeyer08
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heath+Emerson"}> Heath Emerson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Meyer"} Ralf Meyer
              = succeed "," do
                %em Bivariant K-theory via correspondences
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0812.4949"} arXiv:0812.4949
          %p
            The point of view that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+as+a+pull-push+transform"} pull-push quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten theory
            should be thought of as a theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+motives"} Chow motives
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stacks"} Deligne-Mumford stacks
            is expressed in
          %ul#BehrendManin95
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kai+Behrend"}> Kai Behrend
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Manin"} Yuri Manin
              %em Stacks of Stable Maps and Gromov-Witten Invariants
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/alg-geom/9506023"}> arXiv:alg-geom/9506023
              )
          %ul#Toen00
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+To%C3%ABn"}> Bertrand Toën
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On motives for Deligne-Mumford stacks
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0006160"} arXiv:math/0006160
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00773027"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/docs/00/77/30/27/PDF/motdm.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The proposal that the natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            for geometric quantization is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaas+Landsman"}> Klaas Landsman
                ,
                %em Functorial quantization and the Guillemin-Sternberg conjecture
                Proc. Bialowieza 2002 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0307059"}> arXiv:math-ph/0307059
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaas+Landsman"}> Klaas Landsman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Functoriality of quantization: a KK-theoretic approach
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.academia.edu/1992202/Functoriality_of_quantization_a_KK-theoretic_approach"} web
          %p
            The point of view that pull-push along
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+K-theory"} operator K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            is a
            = succeed "-analog" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives"} motives
            was amplified in
          %ul#ConnesSkandalis84
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Skandalis"} Georges Skandalis
              = succeed "." do
                %em The longitudinal index theorem for foliations
              %p
                no. 6, 1139–1183 (1984) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.alainconnes.org/docs/longitudinal.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %ul#ConnesConsaniMarcolli05
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              , Caterina Consani,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em Noncommutative geometry and motives: the thermodynamics of endomotives
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512138"}> arXiv:math/0512138
              )
          %p
            The proof that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            that characterizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
            is the analog in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            that characterizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+topology"} noncommutative topology
            is due to
          %ul#Tabuada08
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gon%C3%A7alo+Tabuada"}> Gonçalo Tabuada
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em K-theory via universal invariants
          %ul#CisinskiTabuada11
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gon%C3%A7alo+Tabuada"} Gonçalo Tabuada
              = succeed "." do
                %em Non connective K-theory via universal invariants
              %p
                Mathematica 147 (2011), 1281–1320 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0903.3717"}> arXiv:0903.3717
                )
          %ul#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Blumberg"}> Andrew Blumberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Gepner"} David Gepner
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gon%C3%A7alo+Tabuada"} Gonçalo Tabuada
              = succeed "," do
                %em A universal characterization of higher algebraic K-theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.2282"} arXiv:1001.2282
          %p
            The description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+topology"} string topology
            operations as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HQFT"} HQFT
            defined by pull-push transforms in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            was originally realized in
          %ul#Tamanoi07
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralph+Cohen"}> Ralph Cohen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Veronique+Godin"} Veronique Godin
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Polarized+View+of+String+Topology"} A Polarized View of String Topology
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0303003"}> arXiv:math/0303003
                )
            %li
              %p
                Hirotaka Tamanoi,
                %em Loop coproducts in string topology and triviality of higher genus TQFT operations
                (2007) (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1276"}> arXiv:0706.1276
                )
          %p
            A detailed discussion and generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+strings"} open strings
            and an open-closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HQFT"} HQFT
            in the presence of a single space-filling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            is in
          %ul#Godin
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Veronique+Godin"}> Veronique Godin
              ,
              %em Higher string topology operations
              (2007)(
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0711.4859"}> arXiv:0711.4859
              )
          %p
            and for arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Kupers"}> Alexander Kupers
              ,
              %em String topology operations
              MS thesis (2011) (
              %a{:href => "http://math.stanford.edu/~kupers/thesis7thjune2011.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            That
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge"} D-brane charge
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            is naturally understood in terms of pull-push/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indices"} indices
            along
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+topology"} noncommutative topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            was amplified in
          %ul#BMRS2
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacek+Brodzki"}> Jacek Brodzki
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Varghese+Mathai"} Varghese Mathai
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Rosenberg"} Jonathan Rosenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Szabo"} Richard Szabo
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noncommutative correspondences, duality and D-branes in bivariant K-theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0708.2648"} arXiv:0708.2648
          %p
            The general analogy between such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            cocycles and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motives"} pure motives
            is noted explicitly in
          %ul#ConnesConsaniMarcolli05
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              , Caterina Consani,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em Noncommutative geometry and motives: the thermodynamics of endomotives
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512138"}> arXiv:math/0512138
              )
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noncommutative+Geometry%2C+Quantum+Fields+and+Motives"} Noncommutative Geometry, Quantum Fields and Motives
          %p This analogy is given a precise form in
          %ul#Mahanta13
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Snigdhayan+Mahanta"}> Snigdhayan Mahanta
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Higher nonunital Quillen
                  = succeed "-theory," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo ′
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K'
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://wwwmath.uni-muenster.de/u/snigdhayan.mahanta/papers/KQ.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://wwwmath.uni-muenster.de/u/snigdhayan.mahanta/papers/KKTD.pdf"} talk notes
          %p
            where it is shown that there is a universal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KK
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi NCC
                    %mi dg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KK \longrightarrow NCC_{dg}
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            to the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-categories"} dg-categories
            and dg-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            up to homotopy between them. This is given by sending a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+complexes"} perfect complexes
            of (the unitalization of) its underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
          %p
            Linearization of correspondences of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+groupoid"} geometrically discrete groupoids
            was considered in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Dolan"} Jim Dolan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Groupoidification
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/groupoidification/"} web
          %p
            and applied to a pull-push quantization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            in
          %ul#Morton10
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffrey+Morton"}> Jeffrey Morton
                ,
                %em 2-Vector Spaces and Groupoids
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.2361"}> arXiv:0810.2361
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffrey+Morton"}> Jeffrey Morton
                ,
                %em Cohomological Twisting of 2-Linearization and Extended TQFT
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.5603v4"}> arXiv:1003.5603v4
                )
          %p
            following previous work by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"} Daniel Freed
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Quinn"} Frank Quinn
          %p An unpublished predecessor note on quantization of correspondences of moduli stacks of fields is
          %ul#Schreiber08
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Nonabelian+cocycles+and+their+quantum+symmetries"} Nonabelian cocycles and their quantum symmetries
              (2008)
          %p
            Quantization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            of
            %span.newWikiWord
              perfect ∞-stacks
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/perfect+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ?
            by pull-push of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"}> David Ben-Zvi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Francis"} John Francis
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Nadler"} David Nadler
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} Integral Transforms and Drinfeld Centers in Derived Algebraic Geometry
                , J. Amer. Math. Soc.
                %strong 23
                (2010), no. 4, 909-966, (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0805.0157"}> arXiv:0805.0157
                )
          %p
            Linearization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+correspondences"} higher correspondences
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} discrete ∞-groupoids
            as the quantizaton of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Dijkgraaf-Witten+theories"} ∞-Dijkgraaf-Witten theories
            is indicated in section 3 and 8 of
          %ul#FreedHopkinsLurieTeleman09
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Mike Hopkins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
              = succeed "A" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"}> Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
                  , in P. Kotiuga (ed.),
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0905.0731"} arXiv:0905.0731
          %p and in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ambidexterity+in+K%28n%29-Local+Stable+Homotopy+Theory"} Ambidexterity in K(n)-Local Stable Homotopy Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/Ambidexterity.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A clear picture of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} fiber integration
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            is developed in
          %ul#ABG
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Ando"}> Matthew Ando
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Blumberg"} Andrew Blumberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Gepner"} David Gepner
              = succeed "," do
                %em Twists of K-theory and TMF
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Rosenberg"} Jonathan Rosenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Superstrings, Geometry, Topology, and
                  = succeed "-algebras" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/bookstore-getitem/item=PSPUM-81"} vol 81
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1002.3004"} arXiv:1002.3004
          %p
            A proposal to axiomatize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+field+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            by functors from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+category"} symplectic category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            is in
          %ul#CattaneoMnevReshetikhin12
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Mnev"} Pavel Mnev
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Reshetikhin"} Nicolai Reshetikhin
              %em Classical and quantum Lagrangian field theories with boundary
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0239"}> arXiv:1207.0239
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://users.uoa.gr/~iandroul/GAP%202012%20WEBPAGE/GAP2012_Cattaneo.pdf"} pdf slides
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Damien+Calaque"}> Damien Calaque
              ,
              %em Lagrangian structures on mapping stacks and semi-classical TFTs
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1306.3235"}> arXiv:1306.3235
              )
